1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,762
A golden moon in a deep blue sky.

2
00:00:02,762 --> 00:00:08,556
Below it, a seashore planted as far as the eye can see with jade green watermelons.

3
00:00:08,556 --> 00:00:16,764
And in the middle, a boy of eleven in a silver necklet, stabbing with a pitchfork at a small wild animal that slips between his legs.

4
00:00:16,967 --> 00:00:25,862
Thirty years later, the same boy, now a worn out farmer, stands in front of his old friend, and says only one word.

5
00:00:25,862 --> 00:00:26,384
Master.

6
00:00:31,867 --> 00:00:38,254
Welcome to Class 12 English, Unit 5: My Old Home, by the Chinese writer Lu Xun.

7
00:00:40,633 --> 00:00:41,754
Six parts.

8
00:00:41,754 --> 00:00:43,708
Lu Xun and his China.

9
00:00:43,708 --> 00:00:45,869
The homecoming, and a memory.

10
00:00:45,869 --> 00:00:48,226
Runtu, the boy and the man.

11
00:00:48,226 --> 00:00:49,293
The other people.

12
00:00:49,293 --> 00:00:50,465
The themes.

13
00:00:50,465 --> 00:00:54,244
And the story's two great images: the wall, and the road.

14
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:04,435
Lu Xun is the pen name of Zhou Shuren, the founder of modern Chinese literature.

15
00:01:04,435 --> 00:01:12,990
Born in eighteen eighty one into a once wealthy family, he spent his boyhood between pawnshops and pharmacies after its collapse.

16
00:01:12,990 --> 00:01:22,833
He went to Japan to study medicine, then gave it up, deciding that his people needed healthy minds more than healthy bodies, and that literature could help.

17
00:01:22,833 --> 00:01:29,495
His story A Madman's Diary was the first modern story written in everyday spoken Chinese.

18
00:01:29,700 --> 00:01:32,709
My Old Home was written in nineteen twenty one.

19
00:01:32,709 --> 00:01:38,009
Like its narrator, Lu Xun went home in nineteen nineteen to sell the family house.

20
00:01:38,009 --> 00:01:44,224
But it is fiction based on experience: in the exam, call the speaker the narrator, not Lu Xun.

21
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:56,918
The narrator travels more than seven hundred miles by boat, in the depth of winter, to the home he left twenty years before.

22
00:01:56,918 --> 00:02:01,892
A grey sky, a cold wind, a few lifeless villages.

23
00:02:01,892 --> 00:02:03,806
Is this the place he remembered?

24
00:02:03,806 --> 00:02:08,780
Perhaps it was always like this, he decides; only his mood has changed.

25
00:02:08,780 --> 00:02:17,282
He has come to say goodbye: the old house has been sold, and he is taking his mother and his little nephew Hongr to live where he now works.

26
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:22,538
Then his mother mentions Runtu, who always asks after him.

27
00:02:22,538 --> 00:02:29,543
At that name a picture flashes into his mind: the golden moon, the watermelons, the boy with the pitchfork.

28
00:02:29,543 --> 00:02:40,090
Thirty years ago, when the family was still rich, Runtu, the son of a part time labourer, came to help guard the ritual vessels at a great sacrifice.

29
00:02:40,090 --> 00:02:44,791
He wore a silver necklet, because his father had pledged him to the gods.

30
00:02:44,791 --> 00:02:47,877
Within hours the two boys were fast friends.

31
00:02:47,877 --> 00:02:56,913
Runtu knew wonders: how to trap birds in the snow, where to find shells of every colour, fish that jumped on two legs.

32
00:02:56,913 --> 00:03:02,728
The town boys, by contrast, only ever saw the four corners of sky above their courtyard wall.

33
00:03:02,933 --> 00:03:07,817
Notice the colours: gold and jade in the memory, grey in the present.

34
00:03:07,817 --> 00:03:12,564
Lu Xun sets a warm memory beside a cold fact, and lets the gap speak.

35
00:03:17,333 --> 00:03:25,434
One cold afternoon Runtu arrives, and the narrator knows him at once, and yet this is not the Runtu he remembered.

36
00:03:25,434 --> 00:03:32,986
The boy was about eleven, round faced and crimson cheeked, in a silver necklet, brave with a pitchfork.

37
00:03:32,986 --> 00:03:45,263
The man is about forty: his face sallow and lined, his eyes swollen and red from the sea wind, one thin padded jacket against the cold, his hands cracked like the bark of a pine tree.

38
00:03:45,467 --> 00:03:52,265
The narrator is overjoyed and tongue tied; all the old talk of shells and birds sticks in his throat.

39
00:03:52,265 --> 00:03:54,909
Runtu's lips move without a sound.

40
00:03:54,909 --> 00:03:58,099
Then, respectfully, he says: Master.

41
00:03:58,099 --> 00:04:04,319
And the narrator shudders, because he knows at once what a lamentably thick wall has grown up between them.

42
00:04:05,533 --> 00:04:08,334
Asked about his life, Runtu shakes his head.

43
00:04:08,334 --> 00:04:13,634
Too many children, bad harvests, taxes, and everyone wanting money.

44
00:04:13,634 --> 00:04:16,785
His face does not move, like a stone statue.

45
00:04:16,785 --> 00:04:29,623
After he goes, the narrator and his mother count what has crushed him: many children, famines, taxes, soldiers, bandits, officials and gentry, squeezing him as dry as a mummy.

46
00:04:29,623 --> 00:04:31,264
There is no single villain.

47
00:04:31,264 --> 00:04:33,083
The pressure comes from the whole system.

48
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:46,307
Offered whatever the family will not take, Runtu chooses two tables, four chairs, a balance, the ashes from the stove for his fields, and an incense burner with candlesticks.

49
00:04:46,307 --> 00:04:48,078
Remember those candlesticks.

50
00:04:48,078 --> 00:04:52,274
The narrator smiles at them now, and will turn the joke on himself at the end.

51
00:04:57,067 --> 00:04:58,071
The other people.

52
00:04:58,071 --> 00:05:07,238
Mrs Yang, once the Beancurd Beauty of the shop opposite, now stands with legs apart like the compass in a geometry box.

53
00:05:07,238 --> 00:05:12,121
She insists the narrator is rich, and walks off with his mother's gloves.

54
00:05:12,121 --> 00:05:18,696
She is comic, but Lu Xun does not condemn her: she too lives by picking up what others leave.

55
00:05:18,696 --> 00:05:23,248
The narrator himself is gentle and honest about his own failings.

56
00:05:23,467 --> 00:05:32,596
And two boys: Hongr, the narrator's nephew, and Shuisheng, Runtu's son, the image of young Runtu without the necklet.

57
00:05:32,596 --> 00:05:36,945
They become friends at once, exactly as their elders once did.

58
00:05:36,945 --> 00:05:45,611
When the mother asks Runtu to call her son brother, as he used to, Runtu refuses: he was a child then and did not understand.

59
00:05:50,400 --> 00:05:51,326
Themes.

60
00:05:51,326 --> 00:06:02,667
The barrier of class is the centre: a thick wall made of money, habit and deference, invisible but high, and built by society, not by either man.

61
00:06:02,667 --> 00:06:07,797
Memory and reality: the golden memory against the grey present.

62
00:06:07,797 --> 00:06:11,769
Rural poverty: a peasant squeezed by a whole system.

63
00:06:11,769 --> 00:06:18,199
And hope in the next generation: the boys' friendship shows that the wall is not natural, and might come down.

64
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:22,032
Then the narrator catches himself.

65
00:06:22,032 --> 00:06:28,868
He had smiled at Runtu for wanting an incense burner and candlesticks, still worshipping idols.

66
00:06:28,868 --> 00:06:35,795
But what is his own hope for a new life, except an idol he has made himself, only further away?

67
00:06:35,795 --> 00:06:40,100
It is irony turned on himself, and it is what makes the ending honest.

68
00:06:44,900 --> 00:06:51,685
As the boat pulls away, the narrator feels no regret, only that invisible high wall.

69
00:06:51,685 --> 00:06:55,253
The picture of the boy among the watermelons blurs.

70
00:06:55,253 --> 00:07:03,378
He hopes Hongr and Shuisheng will never be parted, and that they will have a new life, unlike his and unlike Runtu's.

71
00:07:03,378 --> 00:07:12,688
Half asleep, he sees the jade green shore and the golden moon once more, and thinks: hope is like a road across the earth.

72
00:07:12,688 --> 00:07:14,563
To begin with there was no road.

73
00:07:14,563 --> 00:07:17,724
But when many people pass one way, a road is made.

74
00:07:17,933 --> 00:07:19,263
It is not a promise.

75
00:07:19,263 --> 00:07:24,354
It is a call to action: hope exists only if enough people walk it into being.

76
00:07:24,354 --> 00:07:33,132
And notice the frame: the same golden moon that opened the memory now closes the story, the past turned into a dream of the future.

77
00:07:34,333 --> 00:07:35,767
Devices to quote.

78
00:07:35,767 --> 00:07:40,702
A frame: the golden moon at the start of the memory and again at the end.

79
00:07:40,702 --> 00:07:43,462
Contrast, the story's main method.

80
00:07:43,462 --> 00:07:49,686
Similes that are harsh and exact: hands like pine bark, a woman like a compass.

81
00:07:49,686 --> 00:07:53,697
Symbols: the wall, the necklet, the idols, the road.

82
00:07:53,697 --> 00:07:55,650
Irony turned on the narrator.

83
00:07:55,650 --> 00:07:58,956
And metaphor: the thick wall, and hope as a road.

84
00:08:01,367 --> 00:08:02,137
Pause and think.

85
00:08:09,433 --> 00:08:15,459
The habit of deference shows the invisible but high wall that poverty and class have built.

86
00:08:17,433 --> 00:08:17,868
One more.

87
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:30,258
There were no roads at first; when many people pass one way, a road is made.

88
00:08:32,433 --> 00:08:33,502
Quick recap.

89
00:08:33,502 --> 00:08:36,562
Lu Xun, and a story from nineteen twenty one.

90
00:08:36,562 --> 00:08:39,140
A grey homecoming and a golden memory.

91
00:08:39,140 --> 00:08:43,606
A friend who now says Master, and the thick wall of class.

92
00:08:43,606 --> 00:08:48,051
And a hope that is honest about itself: a road made by walking.

93
00:08:52,233 --> 00:08:53,654
That was My Old Home.

94
00:08:53,654 --> 00:09:00,685
Next, a story from Nepal: The Half-closed Eyes of the Buddha and the Slowly Sinking Sun.

95
00:09:00,685 --> 00:09:01,238
See you there.

